A||||7||Punkndrublic||Jacobs Electronics Ignition
Z||000000||Punkndrublic||08-08-99||04:51 PM||||I just bought a new ignition from jacobs electronics. Its the mileage master kit. The kit has an iginition box(computer brain), coil, spark plug wires, all the stuff to hook it up. The whole setup cost me 550 cdn after taxes and didn't take very long to hook up, the part that took the longest was cutting and making the spark plug wires and figuring out where to put everything. I noticed a definate increase in performance especailly in the higher rpms. It runs smoother and some power power. I haven't gapped the plugs yet like im supposed to, which is also supposed to increase power as well. But as far as everything goes im really happy with everything. Ill be buying their gapping tool just to make the job easier.
Z||000001||Cooter||08-08-99||09:23 PM||||I have a friend who had the jacobs box on his car. Dont leave the wiring in such a way that you cant revert back to the factory coil and ignition. His box died and he would have been stranded if not for being able to disconnect it and run on the factory system. That was 3 months ago and jacobs has not sent a replacement yet. 
Z||000002||Kage86GT4SP||08-08-99||10:33 PM||||Well, I put in an MSD 5 box and MSD Blaster coil.  REALLY smooth now... I also got the optional little GM wiring harness that they have (even though those 4 wires and 2 plug ins cost a bundle ($30)) for a great plug-in install.<p>Total cost was $80 for the box, $40 for the coil and $30 for the harness.<p>I just couldn't cough up the $389 they wanted for the Jacobs, which, as I understand it, is MSD based anyway.<p>K
Z||000003||Punkndrublic||08-08-99||10:40 PM||||Well as far as i know msd is old school stuff from the 70's and not nearly as good. the jacobs stuff just came out round here and all the parts guys are impressed and buying them, so i thought what the hell if these guys are happy then i ll be happy too.
Z||000004||Cooter||08-09-99||06:39 AM||||I've got a car magazine around here somewhere that compares all the aftermarket ignition boxes. The MSD came out on top followed by the Holley, Mallory and the Jacobs was near the bottom. All test performed on a stock engine with no changes except the box and whatever the manufacturer recomemded. Sometimes 70's technology aint so bad.
Z||000005||PaUL||08-09-99||06:45 PM||||Talking about 70s tech, let me tell you about my ignition system. Its not just 70s technology, it really IS from the 70s, manufactured before 1980. Im using and old style HEI distributer with the coil in the cap. Im not using any kind of ECM at all. The distributor has vaccuum and mechanical advance with no ECM connections. I replaced the original coil with an Accel supercoil and plan to replace the module with one from MSD. I like this system, all the parts are contained within the distributor. The only thing I don't like about it is the size, its kind of big and looks strange on top of the 2.8. 
Z||000006||Patrick||08-09-99||06:57 PM||||<br>Paul, that's the same distributor that I have on my '78 Z28 and it works great. The 350 in that car has been hopped up a bit with a semi-wild cam and it revs beautifully with no missing at upper RPMs. And the plugs also stay clean which I consider to be a big plus.<p>Sometimes the "old" stuff is pretty good!<p>
Z||000007||PaUL||08-09-99||07:04 PM||||Yeah thats why its so big. It was originally designed for V8s. I guess when GM first started making the 60deg 2.8s in 79, they just used the parts they had already designed but slightly modified them to fit the v6. 
